('489e131c-dce5-5214-9ceb-e7b6209d942e'::uuid,'est','"Sequences, Complex Numbers & Advanced Algebra"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Which of the following statements describes the total of the first n terms of the arithmetic sequence below?\n1, 3, 5, 7, 9, …"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"The total is always equal to 25 regardless of n."},{"key":"G","text":"The total is always equal to 2n."},{"key":"H","text":"The total is always equal to 3n."},{"key":"J","text":"The total is always equal to n²."},{"key":"K","text":"There is no consistent pattern for the total."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t9.md","source_sha256":"417d165758ab4ea90b91891cea0b6cd71d4ad113c1cbac2ccd7241a361c29d57","source_question_id":"T9-46","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Sequences, Complex Numbers & Advanced Algebra"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-46)."'::jsonb),
('58b623d1-753f-5c18-a0a4-0197c43dd050'::uuid,'est','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A playground is (x + 7) units long and (x + 3) units wide. If a square of side length x is sectioned off from the playground to make a sandpit, which of the following could be the remaining area of the playground?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"x² + 10x + 21"},{"key":"G","text":"10x + 21"},{"key":"H","text":"2x + 10"},{"key":"J","text":"21x"},{"key":"K","text":"21"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t9.md","source_sha256":"417d165758ab4ea90b91891cea0b6cd71d4ad113c1cbac2ccd7241a361c29d57","source_question_id":"T9-47","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-47)."'::jsonb),
('27f99ca1-5e83-5f01-ae40-1c812b7e124b'::uuid,'est','"Functions, Transformations & Graphs"'::jsonb,'hard','mcq','"If f(x) = (1/3)x + 13 and g(x) = 3x² + 6x + 12, which expression represents f(g(x))?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"x² + 12x + 4"},{"key":"B","text":"x²/3 + 2x + 194"},{"key":"C","text":"x² + 2x + 17"},{"key":"D","text":"x² + 2x + 25"},{"key":"E","text":"x² + 2x + 54"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t9.md","source_sha256":"417d165758ab4ea90b91891cea0b6cd71d4ad113c1cbac2ccd7241a361c29d57","source_question_id":"T9-48","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Functions, Transformations & Graphs"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-48)."'::jsonb),
('d63c215a-4123-5d8d-9689-ed724f78bdec'::uuid,'est','"Polynomials & Quadratic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"x: −4, −1, 2, 4, 5\nf(x): 12, −3, 0, 12, 21\nWhich of the following is true about f(x), which is a quadratic function?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"(2, 0) is a vertex"},{"key":"B","text":"x = 0 is a symmetric line"},{"key":"C","text":"x = 2 is a symmetric line"},{"key":"D","text":"(4, 12) is the vertex"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t9.md","source_sha256":"417d165758ab4ea90b91891cea0b6cd71d4ad113c1cbac2ccd7241a361c29d57","source_question_id":"T9-49","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Polynomials & Quadratic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-49)."'::jsonb),
('eb743cbb-d692-571d-8a12-fa051cd19f83'::uuid,'est','"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"For all x ≠ −1, which of the following is equivalent to (x² − 5x − 6)/(x + 1) + x + 1 ?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"x − 5"},{"key":"G","text":"2x − 5"},{"key":"H","text":"x² − 5x − 6"},{"key":"J","text":"(2x − 5)/(x + 1)"},{"key":"K","text":"(x² − 4x − 5)/(x + 1)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t9.md","source_sha256":"417d165758ab4ea90b91891cea0b6cd71d4ad113c1cbac2ccd7241a361c29d57","source_question_id":"T9-50","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-50)."'::jsonb),
('4c08e0c5-76c5-5c2a-9e6d-a1111d814e6e'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"In △ABC, BD is an altitude. If the area of △ABC is 84 square centimeters, what is the perimeter of △ABC to the nearest centimeter?\n[Figure: triangle ABC with altitude BD = 12 cm from B to AC, right angle at D; AB = 13 cm.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"42"},{"key":"B","text":"48"},{"key":"C","text":"54"},{"key":"D","text":"235"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-01","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-01.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-01","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-01)."'::jsonb),
('6503f5aa-b1d0-5066-91f8-2b769805f77f'::uuid,'est','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"The table shows a linear relationship between vehicle capacity, in liters (L), and mileage, in miles per gallon. Based on the data in this table, of the following, which is the best prediction of the mileage, in mpg, for a 3.6 L engine?\nTable: Engine Capacity (L) / Mileage (mpg): 1.8 / 38; 2.2 / 34; 2.6 / 30; 3.0 / 26."'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"19"},{"key":"B","text":"20"},{"key":"C","text":"21"},{"key":"D","text":"22"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-02","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-02)."'::jsonb),
('e98afaa7-4853-5c50-9966-d46d17774c16'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Helmut cuts a triangle, represented by △DEF, out of a sheet of brass. Point G is the point on this triangle equidistant from all 3 vertices. Point G must be the center of\n[Figure: triangle DEF with interior point G; segments GD, GE, GF marked congruent.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Mass for the brass triangle"},{"key":"B","text":"The circle that can be inscribed within the triangle"},{"key":"C","text":"The circle that can be circumscribed around the triangle"},{"key":"D","text":"The circle containing all points H, such that D, E, F, and H form a rectangle"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-03","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-03.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-03","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-03)."'::jsonb),
('bf021d55-375c-5557-8941-ab2ff7e45c75'::uuid,'est','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"The height, h(t), of a ball is modeled by the function h(t) = −16t² + vt + s, where s is the initial height in feet, v is the initial velocity in feet per second, and t is the number of seconds after the ball is thrown. Suppose the ball was thrown straight up from a height 48 feet above the ground with an initial velocity of 8 feet per second. Which of the following shows all and only the times, in seconds, it will take the ball to hit the ground after it is thrown?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"3/2"},{"key":"B","text":"2"},{"key":"C","text":"2 and −3/2"},{"key":"D","text":"2 and 3/2"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-04","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-04)."'::jsonb),
('11c376fd-7f7c-5597-b707-d071e6d8891a'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"As shown in the figure, a physics student standing at point A is doing an experiment with mirrors. He wants to determine the distance from point C on the ground to point E at the top of one of the second-story windows of a house. He places a mirror on the ground at point B so that when he looks in the mirror, he can see the top of the window. His eye level, point D, is 6 feet above the ground. He measures and finds that AB = 4 feet and BC = 14 feet. Approximately how many feet above the ground is the top of the second-story window?\n[Figure: student at A with eye level D; mirror at B; house at C with top of window E; right angles at A and C.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"10"},{"key":"B","text":"16"},{"key":"C","text":"21"},{"key":"D","text":"24"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-05","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-05.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-05","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-05)."'::jsonb),
('057e2f38-2aa9-5290-a7e7-38fa80d4a532'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The 3 lines shown intersect at 1 point. What is the value of y? (Note: the figure is NOT drawn to scale.)\n[Figure: three concurrent lines; angles 56°, 7x°, 2x°, y° marked around the intersection; 7x° is the vertical angle of 56°.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"16"},{"key":"B","text":"62"},{"key":"C","text":"108"},{"key":"D","text":"124"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-06","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-06.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-06","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-06)."'::jsonb),
('a8bdbf10-9ae2-50eb-a14c-86b35eb2e6eb'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Points W, X, Y, and Z are plotted in the standard (x, y) coordinate plane. What is the area of square WXYZ, in square coordinate units?\n[Figure: W(3, 7), X(6, 5), Y(4, 2), Z(1, 4).]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"√13"},{"key":"B","text":"5"},{"key":"C","text":"13"},{"key":"D","text":"25"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-07","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-07.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-07","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-07)."'::jsonb),
('ec190476-34fc-5a99-92a4-b0e0ae5ac274'::uuid,'est','"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"For all x > 0, the expression 3x²/(3x³ + 27x) is equivalent to"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"x/(x² + 9)"},{"key":"B","text":"1/(x + 9)"},{"key":"C","text":"1/(10x)"},{"key":"D","text":"1/(9x)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-08","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-08)."'::jsonb),
('09271e3b-65c8-5f3d-8644-cb95f35ed207'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the standard (x, y) coordinate plane, what are the radius (r) and center (C) of the circle described by the equation (x − 4)² + (y + 3)² = 16?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"r = 4, C (4, −3)"},{"key":"B","text":"r = 8, C (4, −3)"},{"key":"C","text":"r = 4, C (−4, 3)"},{"key":"D","text":"r = 8, C (−4, 3)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-09","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-09)."'::jsonb),
('242fc3f9-caba-5824-a13c-183910c9d98e'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the figure, AB and AC are radii of a circle. If m∠A = x° and m∠C = (3x + 13)°, determine the value of x. (Note: the figure is NOT drawn to scale.)\n[Figure: circle with center A; B and C on the circle; chord BC drawn; angle at A is x°, angle at C is (3x + 13)°.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"11"},{"key":"B","text":"22"},{"key":"C","text":"33.4"},{"key":"D","text":"41.75"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-10","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-10.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-10","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-10)."'::jsonb),
('a80b328f-6d02-5738-8fe7-4a87290e617a'::uuid,'est','"Functions, Transformations & Graphs"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Tickets for the school play cost $6 for an adult and $3 for a student. The school wants to sell at least $1,500 worth of tickets. If x is the number of adult tickets and y is the number of student tickets sold, the shaded region in which of the following graphs includes all combinations of ticket sales of at least $1,500?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Graph with shading below the segment from (0, 250) to (500, 0)"},{"key":"B","text":"Graph with shading above the segment from (0, 250) to (500, 0)"},{"key":"C","text":"Graph with shading below the segment from (0, 500) to (250, 0)"},{"key":"D","text":"Graph with shading above the segment from (0, 500) to (250, 0)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-11","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Functions, Transformations & Graphs"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-11)."'::jsonb),
('ac9a9fb8-56ca-52bb-bde3-1cc79b5b9b51'::uuid,'est','"Polynomials & Quadratic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Which of the following expressions is equivalent to the trinomial 6x² + 2x − 4?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"2(x − 1)(3x + 2)"},{"key":"B","text":"2(x + 2)(3x − 1)"},{"key":"C","text":"2(x + 1)(3x − 2)"},{"key":"D","text":"2(x − 2)(3x + 1)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-12","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Polynomials & Quadratic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-12)."'::jsonb),
('fc6df54a-bc5c-5c14-bd64-d4aa2f0f2589'::uuid,'est','"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Which of the expressions is equivalent to x⁻³/(x⁻⁷x⁵) for all x ≠ 0?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"1/x"},{"key":"B","text":"1/x⁵"},{"key":"C","text":"x"},{"key":"D","text":"x⁵"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-13","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-13)."'::jsonb),
('b69c6ef5-9e29-5f85-818a-4b6c7d9aa9e4'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"What is the slope of the line whose y-intercept is 8 and whose x-intercept is −6?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"4/3"},{"key":"B","text":"3/4"},{"key":"C","text":"−3/4"},{"key":"D","text":"−4/3"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-14","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-14)."'::jsonb),
('bf320024-622e-5064-a2d7-6c7b724c7199'::uuid,'est','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"The graph models the number of hours worked, n, and the total pay of a company employee, p. Which equation best models p as a function of n?\n[Figure: line through the origin and the point (3, 36); axes: n = hours worked, p = total pay.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"p = 3n"},{"key":"B","text":"p = 12n"},{"key":"C","text":"p = 33n"},{"key":"D","text":"p = 36n"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-15","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-15.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-15","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-15)."'::jsonb),
('f38051b7-c195-5499-b837-91058254acaa'::uuid,'est','"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"For all values of x excluding −5 and 5, what is the sum of (2/3)·(3/(x + 5)) and (1/4)·(x/(x² − 25))?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"(3 + x)/(x² − 25)"},{"key":"B","text":"(9x − 40)/(4x² − 100)"},{"key":"C","text":"(27x − 5)/(12x² − 300)"},{"key":"D","text":"(x² + 12x − 65)/(4x² − 100)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-16","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-16)."'::jsonb),
('8c8fc29b-8ca0-51d4-91c2-d43895738ba3'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the figure, parallel lines a and b are cut by transversal c. What is the value of x?\n[Figure: same-side interior angles (x²)° at line a and (3x)° at line b.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"3"},{"key":"B","text":"12"},{"key":"C","text":"18"},{"key":"D","text":"38"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-17","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-17.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-17","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-17)."'::jsonb),
('f30645dc-6004-5d0f-992d-0ecbcdf896bc'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the figure, R is the intersection of QT and PS, and lengths are measured in feet. Which of the claims must be correct?\n[Figure: QR = 12, RP = 6, RS = 8, RT = 4; R is between Q and T and between P and S.]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"△QRP ~ △SRT"},{"key":"B","text":"△QRP ≅ △SRT"},{"key":"C","text":"△QTP ~ △SPT"},{"key":"D","text":"△QTP ≅ △SPT"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"extra.md","source_sha256":"84ca68dc45b2ef2b31ffef4bf4fca4ef2cb3beb1365109214d65e6b7240c41ea","source_question_id":"E-18","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/E-18.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for E-18","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from extra.md (E-18)."'::jsonb),
